Each thread keeps a cache of its freed blocks of up to 4 KiB, and malloc
hands them out again without frigg's slab pool, as glibc's tcache does
(ADR 0657). The pool takes the lock of a block's size for every malloc
and every free, one lock that all threads share, and a contended lock
sleeps in the kernel at once; it also moves a slab in and out of a tree
whenever the slab fills or empties. A malloc and a free of small blocks
took 3.3 times glibc's time on one thread on the OptiPlex, and 13 times
on two threads at once.

A cache keeps 16 blocks of each size from 16 bytes to 1 KiB and 4 of
2 KiB and of 4 KiB. When it has none of a size, it takes half that many
from the pool with one hold of the lock (slab_pool::allocate_batch), and
when it is full, it gives the older half back the same way (free_batch).
posix_memalign takes a cached block when the alignment is at most the
block's size, at a multiple of which every block of a bucket lies; a
malloc of 8 bytes or fewer takes a 16-byte block, as an 8-byte one has no
room for the key below. thread_exit gives the thread's blocks back to
the pool before its TLS goes, and its later frees go there.

As glibc hardens tcache: a cached block's first word links it to the
next of its size, mangled with the word's own address and, unlike
glibc, a secret of the process's; malloc checks that the link it follows
is a block of the size, or nothing after the last, and ends the process
if not. The second word holds a key of the process's while the block is
cached, cleared when malloc hands it out: a free that finds it there
looks for the block in the cache, and ends the process if it is there, a
double free. Both words come from getentropy once a process.

The cache lives in TLS, initial-exec, as the C library is always loaded
with the program, so the TCB's layout, which the other architectures'
assembly depends on, does not change. MLIBC_DEBUG_MALLOC turns it off,
since only the pool's path logs, and the debug allocator has none.

